@page "/projects/3d-reconstruction"
@using website.Data

<ProjectPage Url="/projects/3d-reconstruction" Kicker="Academic Project · MEng Group Project">
                <p class="max-w-3xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                    My master's group project at Leeds. Five of us built a pipeline that turns a single video from an ordinary camera into a 3D mesh at real-world scale,
                    with no depth sensor, no second camera and no training on the scene. I led the team and built the stage that ties the pieces together.
                </p>

                <ProjectSection Title="Overview" Icon="fas fa-cube">
                    <ProjectFacts Items="@(new[] { ("Module", "COMP5530 Group Project"), ("Year", "2025/26 · Year 4"), ("Team", "5 students, supervised by Sharib Ali"), ("My role", "Team lead, pipeline integration") })" />
                    <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                        Getting 3D geometry out of a single video is hard. Depth models give detailed depth for each frame, but it flickers from one frame to the next,
                        so the frames don't line up when you stack them. Classical methods line frames up well but only recover sparse points.
                        We combined both: two pretrained models do the perception, and a classical optimisation step makes their outputs agree with each other.
                    </p>
                    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                        The input is one RGB video. The output is a textured mesh, a coloured point cloud and the camera's path through the scene, all in metres.
                    </p>
                </ProjectSection>

                <ProjectSection Title="How the pipeline works" Icon="fas fa-diagram-project">
                    <ol class="space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                        <li><span class="font-semibold text-white">1. Depth.</span> Depth Anything V3 predicts depth in metres for every frame, plus a confidence map and a rough camera pose.
                            Long videos are processed in overlapping 128-frame windows, each aligned to the first.</li>
                        <li><span class="font-semibold text-white">2. Tracking.</span> CoTracker3 follows thousands of points through the whole video, including through occlusion.
                            As the camera moves into new areas, lost points are dropped and new ones are added.</li>
                        <li><span class="font-semibold text-white">3. Geometry.</span> A rigidity filter (iteratively reweighted least squares) removes tracks that don't fit a rigid scene, such as reflections or things that move.
                            Then bundle adjustment refines every camera pose and a depth correction for every frame, so the tracked points project back to where they were seen.</li>
                        <li><span class="font-semibold text-white">4. Fusion.</span> The corrected depth maps are merged into a single volume (TSDF fusion in Open3D), and a mesh and point cloud are extracted from it.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                        Both models run with frozen weights, so nothing is trained per scene. Each stage saves its output, so a later stage can be re-run without repeating the expensive model passes.
                        On a single GPU, a 2,000-frame video takes about 16 minutes from start to finish.
                    </p>
                </ProjectSection>

                <ProjectSection Title="My part" Icon="fas fa-user-gear">
                    <ul class="space-y-3 leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                        <li>• I led the pipeline integration: joining the depth and tracking outputs, writing the bundle adjustment, and adding the rigidity filter to make the optimisation robust.</li>
                        <li>• I was the team lead. Most of the group hadn't covered this area before, so early on I ran knowledge-sharing sessions and helped break down the papers we were building on.</li>
                        <li>• I split the work by what each person was strongest at: tracking, depth, integration, and two people on evaluation.</li>
                    </ul>
                </ProjectSection>

                <ProjectSection Title="Results" Subtitle="Each sequence was run three times on the University of Leeds Aire HPC cluster, and the results averaged" Icon="fas fa-chart-line">
                    <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
                        <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-white/[.02] p-5">
                            <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-white">1.56 cm</div>
                            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-400">mean camera position error across the 8 Replica scenes</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-white/[.02] p-5">
                            <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-white">71.0%</div>
                            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-400">of the reconstructed surface within 5 cm of the true surface on Replica (88.3% within 15 cm)</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-white/[.02] p-5">
                            <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-white">RGB only</div>
                            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-400">no depth sensor, compared with methods that use one</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3 leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                        <li>• <span class="font-semibold text-white">Replica (indoor, synthetic):</span> our camera error of 1.56 cm was in line with NICE-SLAM's 1.95 cm, even though NICE-SLAM uses measured depth for every frame and we used colour video alone.</li>
                        <li>• <span class="font-semibold text-white">ScanNet (indoor, real):</span> short, textured sequences came close to the Replica results. Long sequences that revisit the same room drifted, because the pipeline has no loop closure.
                            Over the six standard sequences our mean camera error was 31.1 cm against NICE-SLAM's 9.63 cm.</li>
                        <li>• <span class="font-semibold text-white">Tanks and Temples (outdoor):</span> the base pipeline struggled with large outdoor depth ranges. We added spatial depth corrections and matched the official evaluation protocol,
                            which raised the share of surface within 5 cm from 0.35 to 0.52 on average, and from 0.29 to 0.60 on the Truck scene.</li>
                    </ul>
                </ProjectSection>

                <ProjectSection Title="Changing course" Icon="fas fa-route">
                    <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
                        Our original plan ended with an AR/VR demo. When we tried it, the fused meshes were too detailed to render at a smooth frame rate, and our supervisor pointed out that
                        the project needed far more rigorous benchmarking. We dropped the VR work and spent that time on evaluation across three datasets instead.
                        We also tightened how we worked once integration got harder: pull requests with branch protection on GitHub, Jira for the Kanban board, Discord for day-to-day coordination and Overleaf for the report.
                    </p>
                    <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
                        <span class="chip">Python</span>
                        <span class="chip">PyTorch</span>
                        <span class="chip">Open3D</span>
                        <span class="chip">Depth Anything V3</span>
                        <span class="chip">CoTracker3</span>
                        <span class="chip">Bundle adjustment</span>
                        <span class="chip">HPC</span>
                    </div>
                </ProjectSection>

